  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-24",
    "title": "Galaxies Battle in the NGC 7771 Group",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  After this galaxy battle, only one galaxy will remain. Already visible in the  featured picture  are  rings  and faint star  swirls  from previous  gravitational encounters . Known collectively as the  NGC 7771 Group , these galaxies lie almost 200 million  light-years  away toward the high-flying  constellation Pegasus . The largest galaxy,  barred spiral   NGC 7771  on the lower right, is itself about 75,000 light-years across, and the galaxy on the upper left is spiral  NGC 7769 , which is almost as large. As these and other  smaller galaxies  make repeated close passages, they will  all likely merge  into one very large galaxy. Playing out over billions of years, the  merger process  is understood to be a normal part of the  evolution of galaxies . Studying  galaxy clashes  like this helps  humanity  to better understand the  possible  merger  between  our own  Milky Way  and  Andromeda  galaxies.   Tomorrow's picture:  open space",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/NGC7771group_Marinoni_1179.jpg?w=1179&h=1422&fit=clip",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-24-galaxies-battle-in-the-ngc-7771-group/",
    "credit": " Roberto Marinoni ",
    "alt": "See Explanation.  Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
